+<p>The <tt>%fallback</tt> mechanism should be thought of as an error-recovery
+technique.  The concept originated with SQLite and the need to maintain
+backwards compatibility as new keywords were added to the SQL language dialect
+understood by SQLite.  For example, when SQLite was enhanced to support
+window functions, it needed many new keywords including PARTITION, RANGE, and
+UNBOUNDED.  But what if there existed legacy database files that contained
+tables or columns with those names?  How would we continue to parse statements
+like the following?
+
+<blockquote><pre>
+CREATE TABLE partition(range TEXT, unbounded BOOLEAN);
+</pre></blockquote>
+
+<p>This conundrum is resolved using a <tt>%fallback</tt> directive similar to:
+
+<blockquote><pre>
+%fallback ID    PARTITION RANGE UNBOUNDED.
+</pre></blockquote>
+
+<p>According to the LALR grammar that defines the SQLite syntax,
+the CREATE TABLE statement above is not syntactically correct
+because of the use of those keywords.  But the <tt>%fallback</tt> directive
+instructs the parser to change the look-ahead token to ID if it encounters
+a syntax error, and try using the ID token instead before giving up and
+reporting an error.
+
+<p>As <a href="https://sqlite.org/forum/info/2026-08-21T19:04:53Z">Sam Atman
+observes on the SQLite Forum</a>, it is possible to get goofy behavior
+with <tt>%fallback</tt>.  Used judiciously, <tt>%fallback</tt> can make
+a parser simpler, faster and more robust.  Misuse or overuse can lead
+to ambiguities, however.
+
