+<p>Think of the wildcard as an error recovery mechanism.
+It is similar, but not identical to, the classic "error" non-terminal
+in Yacc/Bison.
+
+<p>
+The <tt>%wildcard</tt> directive was added to support parsing
+of the CREATE VIRTUAL TABLE statement in the language dialect understood
+by SQLite.  A CREATE VIRTUAL TABLE statement follows syntax like this:
+
+<blockquote>
+<tt>CREATE VIRTUAL TABLE</tt> <i>name</i> <tt>USING</tt> <i>module</i><tt>(</tt><i>...</i><tt>)</tt>
+</blockquote>
+
+<p>The ellipsis in the example means any (parenthesis-balanced) text can
+go here.  The ellipsis can include keywords, operators, identifiers, and
+any other text, as long as parentheses are balanced.  Maybe you could
+specify such a thing in pure LALR(1), but doing so would be tricky and
+fragile (likely to break as new keywords are added) and slow.  By using
+<tt>%wildcard</tt>, the ellipsis gets parsed using the already existing
+error recovery logic.  The grammar is simplified and the resulting parser
+is compact and efficient.
+
+<p>Indiscriminate use of <tt>%wildcard</tt> can lead to a parser with many
+conflicts and/or a parser that do not work as anticipated.  However, used
+judiciously and in the right context, <tt>%wildcard</tt> provides a
+technique to identify a part of the language where any sequence of tokens
+can be inserted, and which is parsed robustly, efficiently, and with
+small parsing tables.  Search for the "ANY" token in the
+<a href="https://sqlite.org/src/doc/trunk/src/parse.y?mimetype=text/plain">SQLite grammar</a>
+to see the case for which the <tt>%wildcard</tt> directive was originally
+designed and implemented.
+
